used argumentative contribution, referencing current discussion, perhaps agreeing or challenging. She has her own notes: Candidate A can anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership, unfriendly, eats unhealthily. Candidate B: very conscientious, handles stress very well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, sometimes adopts wrong tone, pretentious. Candidate C: can make correct decisions quickly, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education, puts concern for others above everything, excellent attention skills. Candidate D: responds to unexpected events adequately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, considered moody, regarded as a loner.\n\nEmily's own notes also include some concerns not yet discussed: For A, unfriendly, eats unhealthily (maybe reliability). For B, pretentious, wrong tone. For C, egocentric, not willing to further education, difficulty communicating. For D, moody, loner.\n\nShe should discuss maybe candidate C as a strong alternative due to decision quality and attention, but communication issues. Or candidate D with responsibility but moody. Or candidate A leadership but unfriendly.\n\nGiven both James and Sarah support B, Emily could either agree or raise concerns. She could point out that B's interpersonal issues may be severe, and perhaps candidate C's decision quality and attention outweigh B's issues. Or she could bring up candidate D's responsibility and problem solving.\n\nShe should reference the current discussion: both have recommended B but note concerns. She could say she is less convinced by B because of interpersonal behavior and propose candidate C as stronger overall due to decision quality, calmness, attention, and concern for others, despite egocentric and lack of further education.\n\nBut need to be careful: candidate C is regarded as egocentric, difficulty communicating, not willing to further education. Those are concerns. However, candidate C puts concern for others above everything, which is good for crew cooperation. He also has excellent attention skills. He can make correct decisions quickly, which is crucial. So maybe candidate C is a strong alternative.\n\nEmily could say: I still vote for B but I have reservations; I would like to hear more about crew cooperation for A and D. Or she could shift vote to C.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Your vote is your current provisional recommendation, not a fixed position.\" She can change after discussion. At this point, she can either stay neutral, but must give a vote. She could vote for B, aligning with others, but bring up concerns. Or she could vote for C, challenging.\n\nGiven both James and Sarah have recommended B, Emily could provide a nuanced view: She agrees with reliability and stress but worries about crew cooperation; she suggests candidate C's decision quality and attention may outweigh B's interpersonal issues. She could vote for C.\n\nLet's decide: Emily votes for Candidate C. Provide reasoning: decision quality, calmness, attention, concern for others, but acknowledges communication and egocentric concerns, but maybe less severe than B's nasty remarks. She can ask for more info on candidate C's crew cooperation.\n\nThus public message: \"I am less convinced by B because of interpersonal behavior; candidate C shows strong decision quality, calmness, excellent attention, and puts concern for others above everything, which supports crew cooperation. While C has communication difficulties and is not eager for further education, his strengths in decision-making and attention are critical for long-distance flights. Therefore I currently recommend Candidate C, pending any additional info on his crew cooperation.\"\n\nProvide metadata vote: Candidate C.\n\nLet's produce."}
